<p>The Trump administration approved more than $8.6 billion in expedited military sales to Israel, Qatar, Kuwait and the United Arab Emirates. The State Department said Secretary of State Marco Rubio determined that an emergency required immediate transfers and waived normal congressional review requirements. The approvals cover missile-defense support and precision-guided weapons after nine weeks of U.S. and Israeli war with Iran. A ceasefire has been in effect for more than three weeks. The package includes Patriot air and missile defense replenishment services for Qatar valued at $4.01 billion, Advanced Precision Kill Weapon Systems for Qatar and Israel valued at $992.4 million each, a $2.5 billion integrated battle command system for Kuwait, and APKWS for the UAE valued at $147.6 million. Several reports said Iran struck Israel and Gulf states hosting U.S. bases after U.S.-Israeli attacks on Iran began on Feb. 28.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/3_z91sZ_5NJ5nRrgNihoNIy8JOX0bA2chy_tZ69cnYmNT5mex14QhvuANkFgsQYI30rSLMkpe9OeIcHJt-tP16Zsktoy8bFbRDb-FnKYnutZqBiHM88s5QuKwaN0H-uXsvWo2r1AsBQseVTf1xsVmuy64zTc24alOsZEJPbJGKGA-7CZZA-nKK_o2JpWOzLDfyhN5pFm' alt='Marco Rubio, the U.S. secretary of state, invoked an emergency provision to allow the immediate sale of weapons to Israel, Qatar, Kuwait and the United Arab Emirates.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Iran diplomacy: Several outlets reported stalled Iran-related diplomacy or a broader deadlock over the Iran war.</li><li>European delays: The Financial Times and Digi24 reported that Washington warned European allies of delays to some weapons shipments as the Iran war continued to draw on U.S. stocks.</li><li>Rights scrutiny: Dawn reported that U.S. support for Israel has faced scrutiny from rights experts over Gaza, while Aaj TV and Tribune cited scrutiny of Washington&#x27;s military ties with Kuwait, the UAE and Qatar over those countries&#x27; human rights records.</li><li>Israel allocation: Ynet said the S